Subject: [PATCH] lora: sx126x: ZephCore native driver

Sole owner of every ZephCore change under drivers/lora/native/sx126x/ --
sx126x.c, sx126x.h, sx126x_hal.c, sx126x_hal.h, sx126x_hal_common.c,
sx126x_hal_stm32wl.c and sx126x_regs.h -- plus the driver's devicetree binding
dts/bindings/lora/semtech,sx126x-base.yaml, which it extends with
lna-bypass-gpios (see "FEM receive-path select" below).  No other patch in this
directory touches those files; see "Why this is one patch" at the end.  The
extension-API header is not patched in: it is copied from patches/zephyr-new/
to the public include/zephyr/drivers/lora/sx126x_ext.h.

== Base driver ==

This driver was forked from upstream v4.4's sx126x, which was then a minimal
blocking LoRa driver, because ZephCore needs an event-driven one carrying the
primitives the mesh dispatcher is built on.  Upstream main has since grown its
own async RX, duty cycle and CAD; how the two now relate is in "Rebased onto
Zephyr main" below.  On top of upstream:

  * DIO1 handling moved off the ISR onto a work queue.

  * RX duty cycle -- sx126x_lora_recv_duty_cycle_async(), filling the
    .recv_duty_cycle_async slot of the lora.h 0.9.0 API, with Rx-gain retention
    across warm sleep (sx126x_retain_rx_gain(), DS Sec 9.6),
    StopTimerOnPreamble, and a two-strike parked-RX watchdog
    (sx126x_dc_watchdog_*) that re-arms the cycle after a false preamble detect
    parks the chip in RX.  sx126x_get_dc_timeout_restarts() backs `get
    dc.restarts`.  Window timing is computed by the caller
    (LoRaRadioBase::startReceive); the driver accepts explicit periods only.

  * CAD as a first-class operation -- sx126x_lora_cad() / _cad_async(),
    SF-scaled detection thresholds (sx126x_cad_detect_peak()), and the adaptive
    tuning hooks sx126x_cad_set_peak_offset() / _cad_base_peak() / _cad_probe().

  * The RX-busy gate the TX path interlocks against: the rx_packet_active latch
    promoted on HEADER_VALID, the SF-aware preamble grace
    (sx126x_preamble_grace_ms()) covering PREAMBLE_DETECTED, and
    sx126x_is_receiving() as the single query -- non-destructive, it never
    clears IRQ bits.

  * CAD-busy in-driver RX restore: sx126x_lora_send_async() accepts entry CAS
    from both REST_STATE and RX, so the LBT branch tracks was_rx and has
    sx126x_restart_rx() put the chip back in RX before -EBUSY propagates to the
    C++ caller.  sx126x_lora_recv_async() has an idempotent fast path, so the
    failure-path startReceive() on that side is a no-op.

  * External FEM receive-path select -- the lna-bypass-gpios devicetree
    property, sx126x_set_fem_rx_enable() and the fem_rx_off flag it sets.
    The line is the FEM's LNA-or-bypass select (KCT8103L CTX on heltec_t096,
    heltec_wireless_tracker_v2 and heltec_wifi_lora32_v43), driven as
    (tx || fem_rx_off) and deliberately independent of the chip enable.
    Backs `set radio.fem.rxgain` (default on = LNA in the path = historical
    behaviour).  Read the "FEM receive-path select" section below before
    touching any of it: this is emphatically NOT antenna-enable-gpios, and
    an earlier implementation that gated that pin instead shipped in 1.17.2
    and cost roughly 69 dB of receive sensitivity.

  * Extension API for the C++ adapter (sx126x_ext.h): sx126x_is_chip_busy,
    sx126x_is_receiving, sx126x_set_rx_boost, sx126x_set_fem_rx_enable,
    sx126x_reset_agc, sx126x_get_rssi_inst, sx126x_get_wakeup_time_us, the
    dc-restart counters, the CAD tuning trio, and
    sx126x_apply_heltec_reg_patch.

In sx126x_hal.c, SX1262 Tx params use a measured-optimal PaConfig table indexed
by requested power (RadioLib PR #1662) instead of the datasheet's fixed
dutyCycle=4/hpMax=7 -- same RF output at lower PA supply current below +22 dBm
-- and OCP is raised to 140 mA, without which the high-power PA is
current-limited to ~14-16 dBm at +22 dBm.

In sx126x_hal_common.c, sx126x_hal_wait_busy() gets a sub-ms busy-wait fast path
ahead of the k_msleep(1) loop (whose scheduler penalty otherwise dominates RX
turnaround), plus a one-shot wake-and-retry recovery before it gives up.

== Band-aware RSSI/AGC calibration (DS Sec 6.1.6) ==

The SX126x RSSI and AGC gain-tune registers are band-specific.  Semtech ships
the chip calibrated for 868-915 MHz on their EVK and publishes a second value
set for 470-490 MHz (DS rev 2.2 Table 6-4, section added Dec 2024).  We wrote
none of them, so sub-GHz builds ran the 868-915 calibration.

That is not cosmetic.  Per DS Sec 6.1.6 an incorrect RSSI produces "an
incorrect gain selection in LoRa and GFSK mode", which "can result in a missed
detection (packet loss) or decreased resistance to interference".

Register 0x08AC is shared: bits 7:2 are AgcSensiAdjust, bits 1:0 select the RX
gain mode.  The driver's 0x94/0x96 constants therefore also wrote the 868-915
AgcSensiAdjust (0x94 >> 2 == 0x25) at every RX-gain re-apply, which would have
clobbered any calibration installed elsewhere.  All four write sites now build
the byte from the cached per-band value.

The low-band column is the datasheet's 470-490 MHz EVK calibration, applied
below 600 MHz.  It is NOT a 433 MHz calibration -- Semtech publishes no such
column -- but it is the nearest published low-band data and beats leaving the
868-915 tunes in place.  A true 433 figure needs the DS Sec 6.1.6 generator
sweep on real hardware.

Applied from sx126x_config() after SetRfFrequency so a runtime band change
re-selects it, and re-applied after each Calibrate(ALL) alongside the existing
CalibrateImage re-issue.

== DIO1 is a plain input: ESP32 light-sleep wake lives in ZephCore ==

DIO1 is configured GPIO_INPUT, as upstream does.  Until 2026-09-26 this patch
passed GPIO_INT_WAKEUP on it and, on ESP32 with CONFIG_PM, registered a
pm_notifier that handed the pad back from the RTC IO mux after every light
sleep (rtc_gpio_hold_dis + rtc_gpio_deinit) and ran the DIO1 handler by hand
when the line was still high.  Both are gone, because in Zephyr's ESP32 GPIO
driver GPIO_INT_WAKEUP means an EXT1 wake, and EXT1 was the wrong mechanism:

  * It takes RTC pads only (GPIO 0-21 on the S3).  xiao_esp32s3 (DIO1 on
    GPIO39), station_g2 (GPIO48) and thinknode_m9 (GPIO42) could not wake on
    a packet at all.
  * On the S3 it has one trigger polarity for all of its pins.  ZephCore's
    pm_esp32.overlay also armed the active-low user button, gpio-keys
    configured it first (lower DT ordinal, same init priority), and the
    driver refused DIO1 ("wakeup polarity conflicts with other EXT1 pins",
    invisible without logging).  Every light-sleeping Heltec from 1.17.1 on
    woke only on its RTC timer and the button, and received by polling.
  * Before every sleep the HAL routes each EXT1 pad to the RTC mux, and
    nothing routes it back.

ESP32 light sleep now arms the radio IRQ line and the user button as level
GPIO wakes (ESP-IDF gpio_wakeup_enable(), any pad, per-pin level) for the
length of each sleep, and re-raises the pin interrupt a sleep swallowed, from
ZephCore's helpers/pm_esp32_wake.c.  Nothing here is ESP32-specific any more.
See ZephCore devdocs/lld/13-power-management.md.

== Bound the lifetime of the RX-busy latch (payload-phase deadline) ==

sx126x_is_receiving() reports "busy" for the whole payload phase from the
rx_packet_active latch, which the base driver sets when HEADER_VALID fires and clears
only on a terminal IRQ (RX_DONE / CRC_ERR / RX_TX_TIMEOUT) or an RX (re)start.

In continuous RX (SX126X_RX_TIMEOUT_CONTINUOUS) there is no symbol timer, so
none of those events is guaranteed to arrive. A HEADER_VALID whose packet never
completes therefore pins the TX gate true forever: the node keeps receiving but
never transmits again until reboot, with nothing in the logs pointing at it. The
duty-cycle parked-RX watchdog does not cover this case -- it is DC-only and
deliberately treats rx_packet_active as a legitimate in-flight packet.

Add header_seen_at_ms, stamped when the latch is promoted, and release the latch
(clearing the sticky PREAMBLE_DETECTED / SYNC_WORD_VALID / HEADER_VALID /
HEADER_ERR bits) once sx126x_max_payload_ms() has elapsed. That bound is the
airtime of a 255-byte explicit-header packet at the current SF/BW with CR 4/8
and LDRO pinned on, plus 25% and 100 ms -- deliberately generous, since firing
early would let TX start on top of a packet that is still arriving.

Arduino MeshCore added the equivalent deadline in 79ef74ea (SX1262) and
0bd871cd (LR11X0); their preamble-phase timeout is already covered here by the
SF-aware grace in the base driver above.

The grace and the payload bound are computed by zc_lora_preamble_grace_ms() and
zc_lora_max_payload_ms() from <zephyr/drivers/lora/zc_lora_timing.h> (copied from
patches/zephyr-new/, shared with the lr11xx and lr20xx drivers and unit-tested on
the host). The CAD_RX probe's SetRx bound is converted with
zc_lora_ms_to_steps24(), which saturates at the 24-bit field: masking it wrapped
the ~273 s SF12/BW7.81 bound to ~11 s.

== Scale the chip-side Tx timeout from airtime instead of a fixed 10 s ==

sx126x_lora_send_async() programmed SetTx with a constant 10000 ms. DS section
13.1.5 is explicit that when the Tx timer fires the transmission is stopped, so
that constant is not a safeguard on slow presets -- it is a truncation.

Airtime of a 255-byte packet at CR 4/8 reaches 17.7 s at SF10/BW31.25, 15.9 s at
SF11/BW62.5 and 28.6 s at SF12/BW62.5. Against a 10 s ceiling those presets lost
every packet from 136 B, 154 B and 76 B up respectively, cut mid-transmission, with
nothing in the log connecting the loss to this line: TX_DONE never arrives, the
timeout handler re-arms RX, and the host wait is what eventually reports it.

Scale as airtime + 25% + 500 ms, floored at the previous 10000 so no preset that
works today gets a tighter deadline. Clamp to 262143 ms: sx126x_set_tx() converts
via SX126X_MS_TO_TIMEOUT (64 steps/ms) and sys_put_be24(), which truncates
silently above that. The ceiling is 262 s against a worst reachable airtime of
~229 s (SF12/BW7.81 at 255 B), so it never binds on a real packet.

== Stop recalibrating the SX126x on every duty-cycle re-arm ==

sx126x_restart_rx() ran Calibrate(ALL) on every duty-cycle re-entry -- which is
after every received packet and after every noise header error -- costing ~5 ms
of busy-wait each time, on the receive path, forever.

Worse, it was self-amplifying: Calibrate(ALL) reverts image calibration to the
902-928 MHz default, clears the AGC band calibration added above, resets the
DIO2 RF-switch role and resets StopTimerOnPreamble. Four of the seven steps in
that branch existed only to repair the damage done by the second one.

The AGC reset itself is NOT being removed. Semtech give warm sleep plus
recalibration as the remedy for a jammed AGC, and sx126x_reset_agc() still does
exactly that. What was wrong was the trigger. Firing on reception is
anti-correlated with need: it runs most often on a busy channel, where the packet
that triggered it has just proved the AGC is working, and no more often on a
silent one, where a desensitised receiver is invisible and nothing else will
reveal it. LoRaRadioBase::agcMaintenance() now drives it from silence instead --
after AGC_IDLE_RESET_MS with nothing heard, when by definition there is no
traffic to lose. Arduino MeshCore reached the same conclusion from the other
direction: its periodic agc_reset_interval ships defaulted to 0 (off).

Per-packet AGC hygiene is unaffected and always came from elsewhere: the RX-gain
write at the end of sx126x_restart_rx() re-applies 0x08AC after SetRx on every
re-entry, which is what actually gives each packet a clean gain state.

StopTimerOnPreamble and sx126x_retain_rx_gain() are kept. The first because
nobody has verified it survives the duty-cycle sleep unaided; the second because
DS section 9.6 states the Rx gain configuration "is not saved when entering sleep
mode with retention activated", making it mandatory rather than insurance -- this
is the one chip of the three where that is true.

== Correct the SetRx timeout constants (DS Sec 13.1.9, Table 13-9) ==

sx126x_regs.h carried SX126X_RX_TIMEOUT_CONTINUOUS as 0x000000 and
SX126X_RX_TIMEOUT_SINGLE as 0xFFFFFF.  They are the wrong way round.  Table 13-9
gives 0x000000 as "No timeout.  Rx Single mode.  The device stays in RX Mode
until a reception occurs and the device returns in STBY_RC mode upon
completion", and 0xFFFFFF as Rx Continuous.  Sec 13.1.9 step 10 says the same
from the other side: "the chip stays in Rx and looks for a new packet if the
continuous mode is selected otherwise it goes to STDBY_RC mode".  The names came
from the upstream v4.4 driver this is forked from; upstream main has since fixed
them the same way.

Every "continuous" RX here was therefore Rx Single.  The chip parked in STDBY_RC
on each RX_DONE and stayed deaf until the work handler re-armed it -- which is
what the comment at the restart site was already fighting ("Restart RX FIRST --
minimise the deaf window").  The LR11xx and LR20xx drivers, written here rather
than inherited, both pass 0xFFFFFF; the SX126x was the odd one out only because
of the transposed name.

This does not touch duty-cycled nodes, which are most of them.  SetRxDutyCycle
carries its own rx/sleep periods, and DS Sec 13.1.7 makes the return to STBY_RC
after each packet mandatory there ("A packet is detected during the RX window, at
which moment the chip interrupts the host via the RX_DONE flag and returns to
STBY_RC mode"), so the duty-cycle branch of sx126x_restart_rx() is correct and
stays.  The presets that reach the continuous path are SF9 and above, where
preambleLengthForSF() returns 16 symbols and the P > 2D+1 gate in
LoRaRadioBase::startReceive() falls through.

One dependency came with it.  DS Sec 13.1.1: SetSleep "can be sent only while in
STDBY mode".  sx126x_set_sleep() writes SET_SLEEP directly, and the call in the
sync branch of sx126x_handle_irq_rx_done() was safe only because Rx Single had
just parked the chip.  Under true continuous RX it would issue SetSleep from RX,
so that site now does SetStandby(STDBY_RC) first.  Nothing in ZephCore calls the
blocking lora_recv() API -- the mesh always passes a callback -- so this is
correctness for the next caller rather than a live fix.  Upstream main has the
same latent issue in its sync path and was deliberately not copied.

== Move the warm-start retention list off the packet path ==

sx126x_retain_rx_gain() blind-wrote a one-entry retention list ({0x01, 0x08,
0xAC}) and was called from both duty-cycle entry points -- including the one in
sx126x_restart_rx(), which on a duty-cycled node runs after every received
packet, inside a deaf window DS Sec 13.1.7 already charges ~1 ms of context
restore to.

The list does not need rewriting there.  It lives in retention memory: that is
the whole mechanism, and it is what survives the chip-internal sleep->RX wakes
its entries exist to protect.  Installing it once is sufficient, so
sx126x_install_retention_list() now runs from sx126x_chip_init(), and again after
the Calibrate(ALL) in sx126x_reset_agc() -- the one operation in the driver that
might plausibly disturb it, and one that is off the packet path.

The write itself is replaced by upstream main's sx126x_add_reg_to_retention(): a
read-modify-write that cannot clobber an earlier entry and enforces the
four-register capacity rather than assuming it, plus a bounds guard on the count
byte so a blank or corrupt value cannot index past the buffer.  The register
constant is renamed SX126X_REG_RX_GAIN_RETENTION_0 -> SX126X_REG_RETENTION_LIST
to match.

0x0889 (Tx modulation) joins 0x08AC in the list, for the same reason 0x08AC is
there: sx126x_reset_agc() performs a warm-start sleep, and without retention the
Sec 15.1 workaround below would not survive it.

Two comments were corrected in passing.  Both claimed the chip "resets 0x08AC to
power-saving on every SetRx command".  The datasheet does not say that -- Sec 9.6
scopes the loss to warm-start wake from sleep only.  The post-SetRx gain re-apply
is kept, being two bytes of SPI that give each packet a known gain state, but it
is insurance rather than a documented requirement and the comments now say so.

== Verify the retention list on air (debug builds) ==

Moving the retention-list write to init rests on the list surviving in retention
memory.  That is documented, but it was not measured here, and the failure mode
if it does not hold is the worst shape available: every duty-cycle window after
the first sleep listens at power-saving gain, 3 dB down, with nothing in the log
and no symptom beyond marginal packets quietly going missing.

CONFIG_ZEPHCORE_LORA_RETENTION_DEBUG (off by default, set by
boards/common/debug.conf) reads 0x029F back and compares 0x08AC against what
sx126x_rx_gain_byte() says it should be.  It runs once at install, and after
every duty-cycle packet from the RX_DONE path -- before sx126x_restart_rx()
rewrites the gain, so the value observed is the one the packet was received
with rather than the one about to be reapplied.  A healthy node logs

  retention[install]: list ok n=2 [0]=0x08AC [1]=0x0889

at boot and "retention[dc-rx]: ok gain=0x.." on the first duty-cycled packet and
every 64th after that; a broken one warns on the first packet following a sleep
and carries a running bad/total tally.

The check costs two SPI reads inside the duty-cycle deaf window, so it perturbs
the timing of the thing it measures.  That is acceptable -- it measures gain,
not timing -- but it is why this is a build option rather than always-on.

== Apply the Sec 15.1 Tx-modulation workaround per transmission ==

The 500 kHz modulation-quality workaround was applied once, inline in
sx126x_config().  DS Sec 15.1 asks for it "before each packet transmission", and
the register is not free-standing: it is lost on any warm-start wake that does
not retain it, and sx126x_reset_agc() performs exactly such a sleep.  Between two
lora_config() calls -- which the C++ adapter's config cache makes rare -- nothing
re-established it.

Extracted as sx126x_apply_tx_modulation_workaround(), matching upstream main's
name and body so it stops being a rebase conflict, and called from the Tx path
alongside the PA-config re-assert already there for the same class of reason.
Only 500 kHz presets are affected; ZephCore defaults to 62.5 kHz, but `set bw
500` reaches it.

== Report an unsupported bandwidth instead of substituting 125 kHz ==

bandwidth_to_reg() and bandwidth_to_hz() returned a silent 125 kHz default for
any enum they did not recognise.  Both now take an out-parameter and return
-EINVAL, matching upstream main's signatures.

This is signature alignment rather than a fix: bw_khz_to_enum() in the C++
adapter normalises to a valid enum before lora_config() is ever called, so the
error path is unreachable from ZephCore and the callers that need a value keep
their previous safe defaults.  The point is that upstream main changed both of
these functions, so leaving ours in the old shape guarantees a conflict on both
at the next rebase for no benefit.

== Two paths that stranded the state machine deaf-and-mute until reboot ==

Both ended in the same place: data->state left at a value no entry point
accepts, so recv_duty_cycle(), recv_async() and send_async() all fail their
entry CAS and log "Busy" forever.  startSendRaw() misreports the resulting
-EBUSY as "channel busy (LBT), re-queuing", so the log blames the channel and
nothing points at the radio.  Only a reboot cleared it.

**1. sx126x_reset_agc() handed the chip back in IDLE.**  Every entry point CASes
from SX126X_REST_STATE, and that macro is SLEEP whenever
CONFIG_LORA_SX126X_NATIVE_SLEEP is set -- the default.  IDLE satisfies none of
them.  The comment at that line reasoned correctly about CAS(REST->RX) and then
wrote the wrong constant; it only ever worked with NATIVE_SLEEP disabled.

The trigger is silence, which is why it presented as "works, then goes deaf
after a while, reboot fixes it".  LoRaRadioBase::agcMaintenance() fires
hwResetAgc() after AGC_IDLE_RESET_MS (60 s) with no demodulation activity, then
calls startReceive() -- which now cannot re-enter.  It also updates its own
timestamp, so it re-strands itself every 60 s thereafter.

Confirmed on hardware 2026-08-21: "agc: 60000 ms without RX activity --
resetting AGC" at 00:07:47.294 is immediately followed by the first
"<err> sx126x: Busy" at 00:07:48.318, and the node neither receives nor
transmits again.

Now calls sx126x_set_sleep(), which sleeps the chip *and* sets state to SLEEP,
so state and hardware agree rather than merely satisfying the CAS.

**2. A 1 s stall in the same function, once per AGC reset.**  First attributed
to the duty-cycle sleep phase holding BUSY on entry, and a raw NSS poke was added
ahead of the first command on that theory.  Hardware disagreed: the stall
survived it completely unchanged.

The real source is three lines further down.  reset_agc() deliberately puts the
chip into warm sleep, then calls sx126x_set_standby() to bring it back -- but the
chip is in sleep *by construction* at that point, BUSY is held HIGH throughout
sleep, and sx126x_hal_write_cmd() waits for BUSY LOW before it will issue
anything.  wait_busy() therefore burned its full 1000 ms timeout on every AGC
reset before the wake+retry recovery rescued it.  A full second of dead radio
each time, logged as "Busy timeout after 1000 ms, wake+retry" immediately after
every "agc: ... resetting AGC" line.

The wake has to be a raw NSS edge instead: sx126x_hal_wakeup() drives it and the
chip initialises into STDBY_RC on its own with configuration retained (DS sec
13.1.1).  sx126x_ensure_ready() already relied on exactly that property, and its
comment already spelled out the trap -- "Cannot use sx126x_set_standby() here
because the HAL waits for BUSY LOW before sending the SPI command, but BUSY stays
HIGH until the chip is woken by an NSS edge".  That is the second place in this
patch where a correct comment sat beside code doing the opposite of what it said.

The entry poke is kept: one GPIO read, and it covers the genuine
mid-duty-cycle-on-entry case that the first theory was about.

**3. A Tx timeout took the duty-cycle re-arm branch.**  Found by inspection
while chasing (1), not yet observed in the field.  sx126x_handle_irq_timeout()
branched on (rx_duty_cycle_enabled && rx_cb != NULL), and on a duty-cycled node
both hold *during a transmit*: the flag stays set across TX and the mesh never
deregisters rx_cb.  A chip-side Tx timeout raises the same IRQ_RX_TX_TIMEOUT,
so it re-armed the duty cycle and returned -- leaving state at
SX126X_STATE_TX and tx_async_signal unraised, so the sender waits forever and
every later entry CAS fails.  Identical end state to (1), reached from the
transmit side.  The branch is now gated on state != SX126X_STATE_TX; a Tx
timeout falls through to the tail, which sleeps the chip (restoring REST_STATE)
and reports -ETIMEDOUT, and the C++ failure path re-arms RX.

== FEM receive-path select (lna-bypass-gpios) ==

Adds a devicetree property, lna-bypass-gpios, and the driver plumbing behind
sx126x_set_fem_rx_enable() -- the radio.fem.rxgain CLI knob.  This replaces the
first implementation of that knob, which acted on the wrong pin and shipped in
1.17.2.

Three of our boards carry a KCT8103L (t096, wireless_tracker_v2,
wifi_lora32_v43), and MeshCore's LoRaFEMControl.cpp for each of them is the
reference for what its two MCU lines mean:

  CSD  chip enable.  HIGH whenever the radio is up; LOW only in sleep --
       MeshCore's setSleepModeEnable() drives exactly this pin and nothing
       else.
  CTX  receive-path select.  LOW routes RX through the LNA, HIGH bypasses it
       (variants/heltec_v4/LoRaFEMControl.cpp spells both branches out in
       comments).  Held HIGH during TX, where the receive branch is inert.
  CPS  TX/RX path select -- not an MCU GPIO on any KCT8103L board.  It is the
       SX1262 DIO2 net, which is why every one of MeshCore's KCT8103L targets
       sets SX126X_DIO2_AS_RF_SWITCH=true and defines no CPS pin at all
       (t096 CSD=12/CTX=41, tracker_v2 CSD=4/CTX=5, heltec_v4 CSD=2/CTX=5),
       and why we set dio2-tx-enable on all three.

Beware the naming inversion between the two Heltec front ends: on the GC1109
(V4.2) it is *CTX* that hangs off DIO2 and *CPS* that is an MCU pin (GPIO46,
"HIGH=full PA, LOW=bypass", don't-care in RX), which is why heltec_wifi_lora32_v4
keeps GPIO46 on tx-enable-gpios and has no LNA knob.  On the KCT8103L the two
names swap roles.  Where GPIO46 still appears as "PA_CPS" in a Heltec pin map
for a KCT8103L board, it is a carry-over from that V4.2 map, not a FEM control:
MeshCore's tracker_v2 target does not define or drive it.

Because DIO2/CPS does the path switching in hardware, CTX enables nothing.  Its
only software-visible job is LNA-or-bypass.  Binding it as tx-enable-gpios
produced the right waveform by coincidence -- tx_enable is driven (enable &&
tx), which matches "HIGH in TX, LOW in RX with the LNA" -- and that coincidence
is what sent the first implementation of the knob to the other pin: with CTX
apparently spoken for, "FEM RX gain off" became

  sx126x_hal_set_antenna_enable(dev, enable && (tx || !data->fem_rx_off));

i.e. CSD low during RX.  That is MeshCore's setSleepModeEnable(), not its
LNA-off state.  A shut-down FEM has no through path: the loss is the LNA's gain
plus the off-state isolation of the part, measured on a V4.3 at roughly 69 dB
(-17 dBm to -86 dBm on a 4 m link) against the ~17 dB the bypass path costs.
Reported by a user who could not raise their own repeater from more than 50 m
away after turning the knob off.

So the pin moves to a property named for what it is.  antenna_enable goes back
to plain `enable` -- the RX/TX/sleep state machine owns it -- and the driver
drives lna_bypass as (tx || fem_rx_off), deliberately independent of `enable`:
while CSD is deasserted this line only decides which path the FEM will select
when it comes back, and leaving it in the receive state across sleep is what
lets an ESP32 GPIO sleep-hold keep the RX path intact.

Support is per-board and automatic: sx126x_set_fem_rx_enable() reports false
when lna_bypass.port is NULL, so the CLI answers "Error: unsupported" on every
board whose FEM gain is not software-selectable.  That correctly includes
wifi_lora32_v4 (GC1109, whose CPS is don't-care in RX -- MeshCore's
LoRaFEMControl leaves lna_can_control false on that branch) and excludes, by
construction, boards with a genuine TXEN on tx-enable-gpios (lilygo_tlora_c6,
lilygo_timpulse_plus), where asserting a line during RX would key the PA into a
receive window.

Audited against MeshCore 2026-08-25.  It ships LoRaFEMControl for six variants;
exactly four expose an LNA knob -- heltec_t096, heltec_tracker_v2, heltec_v4
(KCT8103L branch only) and heltec_v4_r8, all KCT8103L, plus station_g3_esp32 on
a different part (P_PRIMARY_LNA_EN).  heltec_tower_v2 uses a KCT8103L but keeps
the same net as a plain TX/RX select and exposes nothing.  Our three
lna-bypass-gpios boards map onto MeshCore's KCT8103L set exactly; we ship no
board where we claim the knob and MeshCore does not, so there is nothing to
scrub.  heltec_v4_r8, heltec_tower_v2 and station_g3 have no ZephCore board.

The knob also applies immediately now, under data->lock -- the same lock
sx126x_set_rf_path()'s callers hold, since this runs on the CLI thread and an
unlocked state read of RX can be overtaken by send_async() taking the chip to
TX.  It is a bare GPIO with no SPI and no chip state behind it, and a node
parked in continuous RX may not make an RX/TX/sleep transition for minutes --
long enough for the CLI to acknowledge a change the radio had not made.

== Bandwidth-aware CAD detPeak base table ==

cadDetPeak was SF+13 for every configuration.  That is RadioLib's default and
it is a reasonable number, but it ignores bandwidth, and the datasheet does not
justify it: rev 2.2 Sec 13.4.7 gives no table at all, saying only that the
values "must be carefully tested", and defers to AN1200.48 (paywalled).

Semtech's own reference stack does have one.  LoRa Basics Modem v4.9.0,
ral_sx126x.c ral_sx126x_get_lora_cad_det_peak(), brackets on bandwidth:

  BW >= 500 kHz   22 23 25 26 30 31 33 35   (SF5..SF12)
  BW >= 125 kHz   20 21 22 24 24 25 27 27
  below BW125     RAL_STATUS_UNKNOWN_VALUE

followed by a symbol-count correction: -1 at 4 symbols, -2 at 8 or 16, since
more looks at the same correlation reach the same detection quality at a lower
threshold.  We run 4 symbols everywhere and applied no such correction.

The error this leaves is mild on this family -- 1-3 counts per octave of
bandwidth -- which is why SF+13 survived so long.  It is not nil: against the
BW500 column SF+13 is ten counts too sensitive at SF12, and `set bw` accepts
250 and 500.  Contrast the LR11xx, where the same omission costs ~12 counts per
octave and drove field units onto the offset rail.

Below BW125 Semtech declines to tabulate, and BW62.5 is our default preset, so
that gap is where we normally operate.  There we take the BW125 column minus a
constant 1 and let the shared symbol correction apply normally, which happens
to reproduce the old SF+13 heuristic exactly at SF5, SF6, SF7, SF9, SF10 and
SF12, and to differ from it by a single count at SF8 and SF11.

Why not simply keep SF+13, given it was measured right at SF7?  Because SF+13
has a uniform +1 slope and Semtech's measured row does not:

  SF        5   6   7   8   9  10  11  12
  Semtech  20  21  22  24  24  25  27  27    steps +1 +1 +2  0 +1 +2  0
  SF+13    18  19  20  21  22  23  24  25    steps +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1 +1

detPeak is empirical PER data; there is no reason for it to be linear in SF,
and we adopt Semtech's shape for BW125, BW250 and BW500 in this very function.
Keeping SF+13 below BW125 would mean the driver used two different SF shapes
depending on bandwidth -- but bandwidth shifts the level, it does not reshape
the curve.  The sub-125 constant (-1 here, against -7 on the LR11xx) is that
level shift; Semtech's row is the shape.

Field agreement: three SX1262s at SF7/BW62.5 -- a house, a roof and a very busy
mountaintop -- converged to 19/19/20 against this row's base of 20, which this
edit does not move.  The only SF8 datum we have (that hilltop, offset +2/+3
against SF+13's 21) points the same way as Semtech's +2 step at SF8.  Nothing
points the other way.

SF+13 was already a 4-symbol figure (RadioLib programs it with CAD_ON_4_SYMB
citing DS rev 1.1 p.92), which is why it was excluded from the symbol
correction.  The sub-125 row is a raw table value like every other bracket, so
it takes the correction like every other bracket -- that is the whole point of
removing the special case.  Net effect: no behaviour change at the default
preset, a real table where there was none at BW250/500, and a one-count move at
SF8 and SF11 below BW125.

The clamp around the adaptive offset moves from 15-40 to SX126X_CAD_PEAK_MIN/
MAX (12-48) and is now exported through sx126x_cad_peak_min/max().  Both halves
matter.  The ceiling has to cover the widest-band base (34 at SF12/BW500 with 4
symbols) plus the full +12 offset excursion, which 40 did not.  The floor stops
two counts above cadDetMin, pinned at 10 everywhere -- a detPeak at or below the
correlator's own noise-estimate floor expresses no threshold at all.  Exporting
it is what stops the C++ staircase exploring offsets that collapse onto one
peak and reading the sampling noise between identical configurations as
curvature; that failure mode is documented on the LR2021 and was observed on
the LR1110.

sx126x_cad_base_peak_cfg() is the single source of truth so sx126x_do_cad(),
the exported sx126x_cad_base_peak() and sx126x_cad_probe() cannot drift: the
base is what `get cad` prints and what the staircase offsets from, so a base
disagreeing with the peak actually programmed would make every rung a lie.

== Rebased onto Zephyr main (2026-09-19) ==

The pin moved from v4.4-branch (1d9ce23e364) to main (3e8f38faf93), 16,052
commits.  Main had reworked this driver on its own, and this patch was rebased
with git apply --3way (15 conflicting hunks in sx126x.c, 1 in sx126x.h).  The
rebase is deliberately behaviour-preserving ("step A"):

  * Our state machine wins wherever the two overlap.  Main added a parallel
    duty-cycle implementation -- STATE_RX_DUTY_CYCLE, set_stop_timer_on_preamble,
    set_rx_duty_cycle, rx_restart, duty_cycle_enter/start/stop, a blocking
    recv_duty_cycle and its own recv_duty_cycle_async.  All of it is removed
    here.  Ours (STATE_RX plus rx_duty_cycle_enabled, the parked-RX watchdog,
    the busy gate) keeps running unchanged, renamed into the API slot above.
    The blocking .recv_duty_cycle slot is left NULL; nothing in ZephCore calls
    it.  handle_irq_rx_done and handle_irq_timeout are our v4.4 versions
    verbatim.

  * Upstream changes that do not overlap ours were kept as upstream wrote them:
    the SF5/SF6 preamble clamp, validate_config, config->sync_word, the CAS in
    the CW-test path, the STM32WL IRQ and PA fixes, .rssi (sx126x_lora_rssi),
    and the .cad / .cad_async slots.

  * Several sections below were written against the v4.4 base and describe
    aligning with "upstream main".  After this rebase that code simply is
    upstream's, and the patch no longer carries it: the SetRx timeout constants,
    the bandwidth_to_reg()/bandwidth_to_hz() error-returning signatures, and
    the body of sx126x_apply_tx_modulation_workaround().  Our second Sec 15.1
    call, inside sx126x_lora_config(), stays next to upstream's in
    send_async.

  * sx126x_add_reg_to_retention() is upstream's function plus our count-byte
    bounds guard.  Upstream still loops "i < n" on the raw count byte, so a
    blank or corrupt 0xFF indexes past its 9-byte buffer.  That is an upstream
    bug worth reporting.

  * The DIO1 work queue is named through k_work_queue_config.name.  Main
    deprecates writing k_work_q.thread directly.

Step B (2026-09-19), each piece benched on a Wio L1 <-> T1000-E PDR run
with duty cycle on, 50 packets each way, all 50/50 (pieces 1-2 at 2 s and
800 ms gaps, piece 3 at 800 ms):

  1. Upstream's sx126x_set_stop_timer_on_preamble() and
     sx126x_set_rx_duty_cycle() restored verbatim and used by both of our
     duty-cycle entry points instead of open-coded command writes.  Same two
     commands in the same order; at the entry site the RX-gain register write
     now precedes StopTimerOnPreamble (all in STDBY).  The duplicate
     SX126X_REG_RETENTION_LIST / SX126X_MAX_RETENTION_REGS defines this patch
     added to sx126x_regs.h were dropped -- upstream defines both.
  2. Our dc_rx_time / dc_sleep_time fields dropped in favour of upstream's
     data->duty_cycle.rx_period / .sleep_period (same 15.625 us units).
  3. Upstream's sx126x_duty_cycle_stop() and its preamble-length explanation
     restored, and used by the duty-cycle entry's cancel branch (identical
     wake-if-BUSY -> STDBY_RC -> sleep sequence).

Net: +2509/-364 -> +2478/-287 lines against upstream.  Deliberately not
done: replacing our STATE_RX + rx_duty_cycle_enabled model with upstream's
SX126X_STATE_RX_DUTY_CYCLE.  Upstream's enum value already sits in the base
file unused, so the switch would shrink nothing, while rewriting the RX->TX
CAS, busy gate, watchdog and restart paths -- the riskiest code in the
driver.  What this patch still deletes is upstream code ours replaces on
purpose: its duty-cycle driver (ours re-arms RX before running the
callback and has the parked-RX watchdog) and its RX-done/timeout handlers.

Open follow-up, not a reduction: sx126x_lora_recv_async()'s cancel branch
(ZephCore's hwCancelReceive()) issues SetStandby without a raw-NSS wake,
so under duty cycle it can block in wait_busy() until the chip's next RX
window (bounded by one sleep period).  sx126x_duty_cycle_stop() would avoid
that, but it is a behaviour change on paths a PDR run does not reach
(reconfigure, CAD-timeout recovery, runtime rxduty toggle), so it waits for
a bench that exercises them.  Validated so far, on a Wio Tracker
L1 and a T1000-E: 50/50 packet parity with the v4.4 build in both directions,
with RX duty cycle off (2 s gap) and on (2 s and 800 ms gaps).

== Why this is one patch ==

Five of the sections above shipped as separate files (0011 band-rssi-cal, 0012
dio1-wakeup, 0013 rx-latch-deadline, 0014 tx-timeout-airtime, 0015
drop-rearm-recalibration), each carrying a note that it was "kept separate from
0003 because that patch cannot be regenerated from the tree".  That was true
while 0003 was hand-written: a git diff of the tree returns the union of 0003
and every patch stacked on it, so 0003 alone could not be re-emitted.  Each fix
therefore became a new file, and sx126x.c ended up five patches deep.

Stacking is not free.  0011 injects sx126x_apply_agc_band_cal() inside the
if (data->config_valid) body that the base driver introduces, so its context
lines live inside another patch's added lines.  An in-place edit of 0003 that
was exactly line-count-neutral still broke 0011 on all three of its files, on a
clean tree with the stamp cleared.  Any reflow of a block one patch adds
invalidates every patch anchored in it, and nothing warns you until the next
configure.

Merging removes the failure mode by construction: one patch owns each file, so
no patch can be anchored inside another's added lines.  It also removes a
duplication that had gone unnoticed -- 0003 was last regenerated as a
git diff -- drivers/lora/, which made it a superset of 0001, 0002 and 0004.  At
configure time 0003 failed git apply --check, the stale-file fallback in
zephcore_apply_patches() reset all eight of its files (discarding 0001 and
0002), 0003 re-supplied them, then 0004 failed and reset sx12xx_common.c to
re-apply a hunk identical to the one 0003 had just written.  The result was
correct only because of that fallback.

The cost is rebasability: an upstream change to sx126x.c now conflicts with one
large patch rather than one small one.  In practice four of the five sections
already touched sx126x.c (only the DIO1 wakeup did not), so a conflict there
always meant rebasing the large patch anyway.  Per-change provenance moves from
filenames into the section headings above.

